    /*
      Every week the same height on paper.

      A table given a total height hands the surplus out in proportion to what
      each row already needs, so busy weeks grew and quiet ones - nearly always
      the first and last, which are part-weeks - stayed squat. On screen it
      never showed: there the cells have a min-height and no total to divide.

      Dividing the height explicitly is what makes them equal, and the row count
      has to come from the markup because CSS cannot count rows - a month spans
      four to six weeks depending on the day it starts on. `height` on a row is
      a minimum, so a week with more events than fits still grows rather than
      clipping somebody's event off the page; at 6.5pt that takes about a dozen
      lines in a single cell.
